 "name": "Barnet, 1471",
 "subtitle": "The ridge north of Chipping Barnet where Edward IV defeated the Earl of Warwick, 1400 – 1500",
 "scopeNote": "A landscape reconstruction of about 3 × 4 km from Chipping Barnet north through Hadley Green to Kitts End, built to show the ground of the Battle of Barnet (14 April 1471). The battle certainly happened somewhere along this stretch of road, but its exact position is disputed: the registered battlefield centres on Hadley Green, while the 2015–18 survey could not find the battlefield and judged that the ground around Kitts End fits the written sources better. Both areas are shown as shaded zones, not as a located site. The blocks of figures are abstract stand-ins on one modern interpretation: they do not show real numbers, real positions or individual people.",
 "origin": {
  "lat": 51.661,
  "lon": -0.199,
  "note": "The middle of Hadley Green, on the old road north from Chipping Barnet (modern A1000), about 850 m north of Barnet church. Chosen because it lies between the two candidate battlefield areas and is the one open space that survives. Local coordinates are metres east (x) and south (z) of this point."
 },
