  "eyots": [],
  "note": "A flat-topped ridge of pebble gravel over London Clay, about 120–135 m above sea level, runs north from Chipping Barnet through Hadley Green towards Potters Bar; the ground falls west to the Mimmshall Brook streams (about 90–100 m) and east into the Monken Mead and Pymmes Brook valleys (down to about 70 m). Heights here are on a local datum: add 64 m to get an approximate height above sea level, so the lowest ground in the scene is about 5 m and the plateau about 65–72 m. The hills and valleys are smooth analytic shapes fitted to a coarse (250 m) grid of modern elevations, with an average error of about 5 m; they are not a survey, and the modern elevation model includes trees, buildings, the golf course and the 1823 cutting on Barnet Hill. The broad pattern of relief is thought to be much as it was in 1471, but no fine detail (ditches, banks, ponds, marshy patches) is modelled.",
  "sources": [
   "bar-eudem-elevation",
